  "B1": {
   "key": "A lens is one fixed question you ask the Core Topic, such as: what does it cost? Each lens gives a different facet.",
   "body": [
    "A <strong>lens</strong> is a question you put to the Core Topic. Ideas come faster through a fixed lens than through free brainstorming, and they are more precise. The <strong>economic lens</strong> asks about money, jobs, prices, revenue and who pays. The <strong>socio-cultural lens</strong> asks about families, communities, tradition, fairness and identity. The <strong>public-health lens</strong> asks about bodies and minds: sleep, diet, safety, stress and wellbeing. Two more are useful for some topics. The environmental lens asks about air, water, land and climate. The political lens asks about freedom, regulation and the duty of the state.",
    "Each lens brings its own vocabulary. Economic: <em>revenue, incentive, expenditure</em>. Socio-cultural: <em>identity, community, tradition</em> and <em>transmission</em> (of customs between generations). Public-health: <em>sedentary, wellbeing, fatigue</em>. This precise vocabulary helps lift Lexical Resource from 7 to 8.",
    "The lens changes the facet, never the Core Topic. The third example below is economic, but it is outside the prompt. That prompt asked about city centres and the right to drive there. After every lens, check the facet against the Core Topic: does it still describe this issue in this setting? If not, the lens has led you off topic.",
    "Then choose. A discuss-both-views essay needs one facet for each view, so give each view the lens that makes it strongest. Use one lens per paragraph. A topic sentence that mixes a health cost and an economic cost has two ideas. In a problem/solution essay, the cause is Facet A and the solution that answers it is Facet B. Both are noun phrases, for example <em>the clash between X and Y</em>."
   ],
   "examples": [
    "Money, earnings and independence: the vocabulary of the economic lens.",
    "Same Core Topic, but a different facet with different vocabulary.",
    "Economic, but outside the prompt, which is about city centres and the right to drive there.",
    "Two lenses in one topic sentence, health and economic, so it holds two ideas."
   ]
  },
  "B2": {
   "key": "Ask the Core Topic one fixed question at a time: what does it cost? what does it do to people and communities? what does it do to bodies and minds?",
   "body": [
    "Ideas come faster through a fixed lens than through free brainstorming, and the ideas that come are more precise. A lens is a question you put to the Core Topic. The <strong>economic lens</strong> asks about money, jobs, prices, revenue and who pays. The <strong>socio-cultural lens</strong> asks about families, communities, tradition, fairness and identity. The <strong>public-health lens</strong> asks about bodies and minds: sleep, diet, safety, stress, wellbeing. Two more are useful for some domains: the environmental lens (air, water, land, climate) and the political lens (freedom, regulation, the duty of the state).",
    "Put the lenses to one Core Topic, such as <em>free public transport in large cities</em>. Economic: the cost to taxpayers of buses that charge no fares. Socio-cultural: fairer access to the city for low-income families. Public-health: the daily stress of driving in heavy traffic. Each facet arrives with its own vocabulary. The economic lens brings <em>revenue, incentive, expenditure</em>; the socio-cultural lens brings <em>identity, community, tradition</em> and <em>transmission</em> (of customs between generations). Public health brings <em>sedentary, wellbeing, fatigue</em>. That precise, domain-specific vocabulary is what lifts Lexical Resource from 7 to 8.",
    "The lens changes the facet, never the Core Topic. The third example below is economic, but it is outside the prompt, which asked about city centres and the right to drive there. After every lens, check the facet against the Core Topic: does it still describe this issue in this setting? If not, the lens has led you off topic, however good the idea.",
    "Then choose. A discuss-both-views essay needs one facet for each view, so give each view the lens that makes it strongest. Use one lens per paragraph; a topic sentence that mixes the health cost and the economic cost is two ideas. In a problem / solution essay the cause is Facet A, and the solution that answers it is Facet B. Write each as a noun phrase, such as <em>the clash between X and Y</em>. Different students choose different lenses for the same prompt, so their essays never read as memorised."
   ],
   "examples": [
    "Money, earnings, independence: the vocabulary of the economic lens.",
    "Same Core Topic, different facet, different vocabulary.",
    "Economic, but outside the prompt, which is about city centres and the right to drive there.",
    "Two lenses in one topic sentence, health and economic, so the paragraph would hold two ideas."
   ]
  }
