  "B1": {
   "key": "<em>Because prices rose, people bought less</em> has two clauses. <em>Rising prices reduced consumption</em> has one verb and two noun phrases: same idea, half the words.",
   "body": [
    "A <strong>clause</strong> has a subject and a verb: <em>because prices rose</em>, <em>people bought less</em>. Nominalisation turns each clause into a <strong>noun phrase</strong> and joins them with one <strong>verb of change</strong>. The cause becomes the subject, the effect becomes the object, and the verb says what the cause did. With only one verb to get right, the sentence is easier to keep accurate.",
    "There are three steps. First, make the cause verb a noun: <em>prices rose</em> becomes <em>rising prices</em> or <em>the rise in prices</em>. Second, make the effect verb a noun: <em>people bought less</em> becomes <em>consumption</em>. Third, link them with a verb of change such as <em>reduce, raise, lower, increase, weaken, drive</em> or <em>cut</em>. Check the form: the head must be <em>growth</em>, not <em>grow</em>.",
    "Now <em>because, since, when, although, so, which, who</em> and <em>that</em> have all gone, because their meaning sits inside the verb. With <em>because of …</em> or <em>… meant that …</em>, part of the idea is still a clause. A relative clause such as <em>staff who commute</em> also keeps one.",
    "However, nominalisation can be overdone. <em>The improvement of attendance was the result of the introduction of a later start</em> has only <em>was</em> to hold its nouns up. This is a zombie sentence: all nouns, no action. The rule is <strong>one nominalised subject, one live verb</strong>. Watch for <em>was the result of</em>, <em>is the cause of</em>, <em>is the reason why</em> and <em>there was</em>. They show that the verb of change has become a noun too.",
    "Use the move on the sentences that carry the argument: topic sentences and the <strong>mechanism</strong>. With the cause as subject and the effect as object, the mechanism reads as a chain without any linking word."
   ],
   "examples": [
    "The cause clause becomes the subject, the effect clause becomes the object, and <em>reduced</em> is the verb of change.",
    "<em>Removed</em> becomes <em>removal</em>; <em>more people used</em> becomes <em>raised … use</em>. There is no when and no so.",
    "Over-nominalised: three nouns made from verbs and only <em>was</em>. The action has been buried.",
    "One nominalised subject and one live verb. The sentence is dense but still readable."
   ]
  },
  "B2": {
   "key": "<em>Because prices rose, people bought less</em> has two clauses. <em>Rising prices reduced consumption</em> has one verb and two noun phrases: same idea, half the words, Band 8 grammar.",
   "body": [
    "A clause has a subject and a verb: <em>because prices rose</em>, <em>people bought less</em>. Nominalisation turns each clause into a <strong>noun phrase</strong> and joins the two with a single <strong>verb of change</strong>. The cause becomes the subject, the effect becomes the object, and the verb says what the cause did to the effect. The sentence is shorter, denser and easier to keep accurate, because there is only one verb to get right. This is why advanced academic complexity is described as phrasal rather than clausal.",
    "The move has three steps. <strong>One</strong>: make the cause verb a noun: <em>prices rose → rising prices / the rise in prices</em>. <strong>Two</strong>: make the effect verb a noun too: <em>people bought less → consumption</em>; <em>students concentrate poorly → concentration</em>. <strong>Three</strong>: choose a verb of change to link them: <em>reduce, raise, lower, increase, weaken, strengthen, drive, prompt, produce, cut</em>. Each head must be a real noun: <em>growth</em>, not <em>grow</em>.",
    "Notice that <em>because</em>, <em>since</em>, <em>when</em>, <em>although</em>, <em>so</em>, <em>which</em>, <em>who</em> and <em>that</em> have all disappeared; their meaning now sits inside the verb. Half-measures keep a clause alive. <em>Because of …</em> leaves the effect as a clause, <em>… meant that …</em> brings back a <em>that</em> clause, and <em>staff who commute</em> is a relative clause. One sentence may carry two verbs of change, each linking a cause to an effect.",
    "Nominalisation can be overdone. <em>The improvement of attendance was the result of the introduction of a later start</em> has three verb-nouns and only <em>was</em> to hold them up. It is a zombie sentence: all nouns and no action. The rule is <strong>one nominalised subject, one live verb</strong>. Writing <em>was the result of</em>, <em>is the cause of</em>, <em>is the reason why</em> or <em>there was a</em> means you have nominalised the verb of change as well. The sentence has lost its engine.",
    "Use the move on the sentences that carry the argument: the topic sentence of each body paragraph and the <strong>mechanism</strong>. With the cause as subject and the effect as object, a mechanism reads as a chain without a single linking word. The rewrite items in this app check exactly this: no clause markers, a cause noun phrase, a verb of change and an effect noun phrase."
   ],
   "examples": [
    "Cause clause → subject noun phrase; effect clause → object noun phrase; <em>reduced</em> is the verb of change.",
    "<em>Removed</em> becomes <em>removal</em>; <em>more people used</em> becomes <em>raised … use</em>. No when, no so.",
    "Over-nominalised: three verb-nouns and only <em>was</em>. The action has been buried.",
    "One nominalised subject, one live verb. Dense and readable."
   ]
  }
