  "B1": {
   "key": "Eight domains, ten collocations each, cover most Task 2 prompts. Learn each kit with the argument it usually serves, not as a list.",
   "body": [
    "Every prompt belongs to one of eight <strong>domains</strong>: education, environment, technology, health, crime, government, work and culture. Each domain has a small kit of collocations that the examiner expects to see. The Band 7 descriptor asks for <em>\"some ability to use less common items\"</em>, and the kit gives you exactly that. However, each pair must be used with its right partner, in its right meaning and in its right domain.",
    "<strong>Education</strong>: foster critical thinking, rote memorisation, vocational training, the national curriculum, lifelong learning. <strong>Environment</strong>: mitigate climate change, carbon footprint, environmental degradation, renewable energy. <strong>Technology</strong>: automate routine tasks, digital literacy, data privacy, online fraud. <strong>Health</strong>: preventive healthcare, a sedentary lifestyle (a life spent sitting), life expectancy, chronic disease.",
    "<strong>Crime</strong>: act as a deterrent, rehabilitate offenders, recidivism rates, law enforcement. <strong>Government</strong>: allocate public funds, an ageing population, a social safety net, impose a ban. <strong>Work</strong>: work-life balance, occupational stress, the gig economy, job security. <strong>Culture</strong>: cultural homogenisation, preserve cultural heritage, brain drain (skilled people leaving a region).",
    "Each pair has one home. <em>Recidivism rates</em> is a strong phrase in a crime essay, but it is a mistake in an essay about exams. Similarly, <em>mitigate</em> means make less severe, so it does not mean prevent or cure.",
    "Learn each kit with the <strong>argument schema</strong> it usually serves. Crime prompts usually turn on prevention versus cure: a deterrent is prevention, while rehabilitating offenders is cure. Health prompts turn on the individual versus the state. To practise, rewrite a health paragraph about crime, or sort mixed phrases into their domains."
   ],
   "examples": [
    "Three crime-kit pairs in one sentence, all on the cure side of prevention versus cure.",
    "Health kit: a sedentary lifestyle belongs to the individual; preventive healthcare and chronic disease to the system.",
    "Recidivism belongs to crime. In an education sentence it is an error, however rare the word is.",
    "Culture kit: the mechanism (homogenisation) and the response (preserve heritage) in one sentence."
   ]
  },
  "B2": {
   "key": "Eight domains, ten collocations each: a working kit that covers most Task 2 prompts. Learn each kit with the argument it usually serves, not as a list.",
   "body": [
    "Every prompt belongs to one of eight <strong>domains</strong>: education, environment, technology, health, crime, government, work and culture. Each has a small kit of collocations that the examiner expects to see and that a Band 6 essay does not contain. The Band 7 descriptor asks for <em>\"some ability to use less common items\"</em>, and the kit supplies exactly that at a level you can control. The rule from the last lesson still holds: a pair works only with its right partner and in its right meaning. <em>Recidivism rates</em> is a Band 8 phrase in a crime essay and a mistake in an essay about exams.",
    "<strong>Education</strong>: foster critical thinking, rote memorisation, vocational training, the national curriculum, compulsory schooling, lifelong learning. <strong>Environment</strong>: mitigate climate change, carbon footprint, environmental degradation, renewable energy, sustainable development. <strong>Technology</strong>: automate routine tasks, digital literacy, data privacy, online fraud, screen time. <strong>Health</strong>: preventive healthcare, a sedentary lifestyle, life expectancy, chronic disease, obesity rates.",
    "<strong>Crime</strong>: act as a deterrent, rehabilitate offenders, recidivism rates, law enforcement, petty crime. <strong>Government</strong>: allocate public funds, an ageing population, a social safety net, public expenditure, impose a ban. <strong>Work</strong>: work-life balance, occupational stress, the gig economy, job security, the minimum wage. <strong>Culture</strong>: cultural homogenisation, preserve cultural heritage, brain drain, cultural identity, mass tourism.",
    "Each kit word also carries a precise meaning. A sedentary lifestyle is a life spent sitting; brain drain is skilled people leaving a region; mitigate means make less severe, so it is not prevent or cure.",
    "Learn a kit with the <strong>argument schema</strong> it usually serves, because that is how you will need it. Crime prompts nearly always turn on prevention versus cure: <em>act as a deterrent</em> is the prevention side, <em>rehabilitate offenders</em> and <em>recidivism rates</em> the cure side. Health prompts turn on individual versus collective responsibility: a sedentary lifestyle belongs to the individual, preventive healthcare to the state. Two exercises fix the kit. <strong>Swap the domain</strong>: rewrite a health paragraph about crime, keeping the schema and changing every kit phrase. <strong>Sort the kit</strong>: mix three domains and separate them against the clock."
   ],
   "examples": [
    "Three crime-kit pairs in one sentence, all on the cure side of the prevention-versus-cure schema.",
    "Health kit: a sedentary lifestyle is the individual side; preventive healthcare and chronic disease are the system side.",
    "Recidivism belongs to crime; in an education sentence it is an error, however rare the word. Kits are domain-specific.",
    "Culture kit: the mechanism (homogenisation) and the response (preserve heritage) in a single sentence."
   ]
  }
