  "A2": {
   "key": "Six families: article, plural, agreement, punctuation, word form, spelling. Find the error, name its family and fix it.",
   "body": [
    "The error auction is a proofreading game. Each sentence has exactly one error from one of six <strong>families</strong> (types of error). Find the error, say its family, and fix it.",
    "<strong>Article</strong> means a, the or nothing. <strong>Plural</strong> means -s or no -s. In <strong>agreement</strong>, the verb matches the head noun. <strong>Punctuation</strong> covers commas, comma splices and capitals. <strong>Word form</strong> means noun, adjective or adverb. The sixth family is <strong>spelling</strong>. Each family has its own place to look: nouns, verbs or commas.",
    "Some errors look like two families. <em>Knowledges</em> is a plural error, not spelling, because the letters are right. The verb agrees with the subject, not with a plural noun after the verb. A noun in an adjective slot, like <em>economy problems</em>, is word form. Use <em>the</em> when an <em>of</em> phrase points to one thing. Amounts like <em>levels of pollution</em> take no <em>the</em>.",
    "A comma alone between two full sentences is a punctuation error. A comma before <em>but</em> is correct. Short words like <em>which</em> and <em>their</em> are easy to misspell.",
    "After ten sentences, count which families you missed. Check your two main families in the last five minutes."
   ],
   "examples": [
    "Plural family: information is uncountable. It is not a spelling error.",
    "Agreement family: the head noun is <em>number</em>, which is singular.",
    "Word-form family: a noun (environment) is in an adjective slot.",
    "Punctuation family: a comma splice, repaired with <em>but</em>."
   ]
  },
  "B1": {
   "key": "Six families: article, plural, agreement, punctuation, word form, spelling. Find the error, name its family and fix it; naming it makes the fix stick.",
   "body": [
    "The error auction is this module's proofreading game. Each sentence contains exactly one error from one of the <strong>six families</strong>: <strong>article</strong> (a / the / nothing), <strong>plural</strong> (countable or not), <strong>agreement</strong> (verb matches the head noun), <strong>punctuation</strong> (commas, splices, capitals), <strong>word form</strong> and <strong>spelling</strong>. You find the error, name its family and give the corrected chunk. The family matters because each one has its own check. You find article errors by looking at nouns, agreement errors by looking at verbs and splices by looking at commas.",
    "Some errors look like two families at once. <em>More knowledges</em> is a <strong>plural</strong> error, not a spelling error, because the letters are right but the noun is uncountable. A plural error can also cause an agreement error, so fix the noun and then the verb. The verb agrees with the subject, not with a plural noun after the verb: <em>The main reason for stress is exams</em>. A noun in an adjective slot, as in <em>economy problems</em>, is <strong>word form</strong>. A comma alone between two full sentences is <strong>punctuation</strong>, but a comma before <em>but</em> is correct.",
    "Articles need care with <em>of</em>. Use <em>the</em> when the <em>of</em> phrase points to one particular thing, but not for amounts like <em>millions of people</em>. Short words such as <em>which</em> and <em>their</em> are often misspelt.",
    "After ten sentences, count the families you missed. Most students find that two families cause most of their errors, and those two are your five-minute check. Finally, if you are unsure, choose the plainer sentence, because a correct simple sentence outscores a faulty complex one."
   ],
   "examples": [
    "Plural family: information is uncountable. It is not a spelling error, because the letters are fine.",
    "Agreement family: the head noun of the subject is <em>number</em>, which is singular.",
    "Word-form family: a noun (environment) is sitting in a slot that needs an adjective.",
    "Punctuation family: a comma splice between two full sentences, repaired with <em>but</em>."
   ]
  },
  "B2": {
   "key": "Six families: article, plural, agreement, punctuation, word form, spelling. Find the one error, name its family, fix it. Naming the family is what makes the fix stick.",
   "body": [
    "The error auction is the proofreading game of this module. Each sentence contains exactly one error from one of the <strong>six families</strong>: <strong>article</strong> (a / the / nothing), <strong>plural</strong> (countable or not, -s or not), <strong>agreement</strong> (verb matches the head noun), <strong>punctuation</strong> (commas, splices, capitals), <strong>word form</strong> (the right member of the family) and <strong>spelling</strong>. Your job is to find the error, name its family and give the corrected chunk. The family matters because each has its own check: articles are found by looking at nouns, agreement by looking at verbs, a splice by looking at commas. If you can name the family, you know where to look next time.",
    "Some errors look like two families at once, and the auction settles which. <em>More knowledges</em> is a <strong>plural</strong> error, not spelling: the letters are right but the noun is uncountable. A plural error often produces an agreement error too; fix the noun and the verb follows. The verb agrees with its subject, not with a plural complement after it: <em>The main reason for stress is exams</em>. A noun sitting where an adjective belongs, as in <em>economy problems</em>, is <strong>word form</strong>. A comma alone between two full sentences is <strong>punctuation</strong>, while a comma before <em>but</em> joining two clauses is correct. For articles, the test is whether an <em>of</em> phrase points to one particular thing; <em>millions of people</em> takes no <em>the</em>. And short words such as <em>which</em> and <em>their</em> are the classic spelling slips.",
    "The auction also shows you your <strong>personal top three</strong>. After ten sentences, count which families you missed. Most students find that two families cause most of their errors, usually articles and agreement. Those two are your five-minute check on exam day, since you cannot read for everything. Band 8 asks for <em>\"the majority of sentences error-free\"</em>, and the fastest route there is to stop making the errors you already make.",
    "Finally, a rule about risk: when unsure, choose the plainer sentence, because a correct simple sentence outscores a faulty complex one."
   ],
   "examples": [
    "Plural family: information is uncountable. This is not a spelling error; the letters are all correct.",
    "Agreement family: the head noun of the subject is <em>number</em>, which is singular, so the verb is singular.",
    "Word-form family: a noun (environment) is sitting in a slot that needs an adjective.",
    "Punctuation family: a comma splice between two full sentences, repaired with the conjunction <em>but</em>."
   ]
  }
