  "B1": {
   "key": "A solution must answer a cause you named, so draw the arrow first. A two-part question gets one equal body paragraph per question.",
   "body": [
    "In the problem/solution playbook, Facet A is the main cause. Its mechanism shows how the cause works and its example where it is seen. Its nuance shows why it is hard to fix. Facet B is the solution. Its mechanism shows how it answers the cause, its example where it has worked, and its nuance its limit.",
    "For city flooding, concrete seals the soil, so rain runs straight into old drains. Parks and surfaces that let rain soak in answer this.",
    "The trap is the <strong>orphan solution</strong>, which answers a cause the essay never named. Test it with <em>\"Because [cause], [solution].\"</em> Cutting carbon emissions may matter, but it does not answer concrete. Bigger pumps are a near-miss: they only move water away faster. The strongest solution breaks the mechanism of the cause.",
    "<em>\"Why has this happened? Is it a positive or a negative development?\"</em> is a two-part prompt with two direct questions. Body A answers the first and Body B the second, with equal weight. Open with <em>\"In answer to the first question, …\"</em> and <em>\"Turning to the second question, …\"</em>. Do not turn it into advantages and disadvantages, or a cause and a solution.",
    "The trap here is one question answered in a single sentence. The descriptor calls this <em>\"some parts more fully covered than others\"</em>. A <em>positive or negative</em> question needs that adjective in your verdict.",
    "Both types take a position in the introduction and the conclusion. For a problem, it is what must happen. For a two-part prompt, it is your overall view, and the conclusion joins the two answers. A solution with an admitted limit is <em>necessary rather than sufficient</em>."
   ],
   "examples": [
    "Cause, then solution: the arrow is inside the sentence.",
    "An orphan solution, because it answers a cause the essay never named.",
    "Body A of a two-part essay: it names the question and answers it with a facet.",
    "Question two is answered in one sentence, so half the prompt is missing."
   ]
  },
  "B2": {
   "key": "A solution must answer a cause you named: draw the arrow before you write. A two-part question gets one body paragraph per question, with equal weight.",
   "body": [
    "The problem/solution playbook turns the matrix into a chain. Facet A is <strong>the main cause</strong>: its mechanism is how the cause works, its example where it is seen, and its nuance why it is hard to fix. Facet B is <strong>the solution</strong>: its mechanism is how it answers the cause, its example where it has worked, and its nuance its limit. For city flooding, concrete seals the soil, so rain runs straight into drains built for a smaller city. Parks, ponds and absorbent surfaces answer it, because water that never reaches the drain cannot overload it.",
    "The trap is the <strong>orphan solution</strong>: a measure that answers a cause the essay never named. The test is one sentence: <em>\"Because [cause], [solution].\"</em> Cutting carbon emissions may matter, but it answers a different cause from concrete sealing the soil. Bigger pumps are the near-miss: they treat the symptom, moving the same water downstream faster. The strongest solution cuts the mechanism of the cause rather than dealing with its results afterwards. Draw the arrow on the question paper, and develop one cause with its answering solution rather than listing three of each.",
    "The two-part playbook is simpler and is failed more often. Two direct questions, such as <em>\"Why has this happened? Is it a positive or a negative development?\"</em>, get two body paragraphs: Body A answers the first, Body B the second, each with the four moves. The frames open with the question they answer: <em>\"In answer to the first question, …\"</em>, <em>\"Turning to the second question, …\"</em>. Reading it as advantages and disadvantages, or as a cause and a solution, drops part of what was asked.",
    "The trap is one question answered in a single sentence. A Body B that calls the change positive and moves to the conclusion has answered nothing; the descriptor calls this <em>\"some parts more fully covered than others\"</em>. A <em>positive or negative</em> question needs that adjective in the verdict.",
    "Both types still take a position in the introduction and the conclusion: what must happen, or the overall view, with the conclusion joining the two answers. Both still take nuance. A solution with an admitted limit is <em>necessary rather than sufficient</em>, and that admission separates a Band 7 paragraph from a Band 8 one."
   ],
   "examples": [
    "Cause, then solution: the arrow is inside the sentence.",
    "An orphan solution. It answers a cause the essay never named.",
    "Body A of a two-part essay: it names the question and answers it with a facet.",
    "Question two answered in one sentence. Half the prompt is missing."
   ]
  }
